[0.005, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;35m━━━ 3. Search: original passages only, no generated answer ━━━\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1.008, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki search price discrimination -k 3\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1.093, "o", "\u001b[1mSearch\u001b[0m (hybrid (bm25 + embeddings, RRF)): price discrimination\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m[P1] raw/micro-final-cheat-sheet.md › Price Discrimination\u001b[0m  \u001b[2mcos 0.63 · bm25 8.1\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  Quantity discount (menu pricing, but for different quantity)\r\n  (1) price for LQ equal to low WTP, (2) price for HQ that makes high type indifferent, (3) Check if\r\n  price is higher if selling only high or low quantity\r\n  When more units, P has to incorporate CS to the first unit\r\n  Condition: seller wants to sell larger Q to the high type\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m[P2] raw/micro-final-cheat-sheet.md › Price Discrimination\u001b[0m  \u001b[2mcos 0.58 · bm25 7.1\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  PRICE DISCRIMINATION\r\n  Charge different prices not due to cost differences\r\n  Goal: capture consumer surplus and eliminate deadweight loss\r\n  First-degree (perfect): Each consumer charged WTP. Rare in practice, requires perfect information\r\n  Third-degree (segment/group): Groups face different prices (ex: students, seniors, geography,\r\n  business).\r\n  Conditions: 1) Different buyers have different WTP; 2) Ability to identify who has high/low WTP;\r\n  3) Ability to segment buyers with different WTP.\r\n  Restrictions: Arbitrage, legal\r\n  Second-degree (menu): offering different versions of a product to introduce lower/higher value\r\n  buyers to reveal themselves\r\n  Downside: Not perfect (CS and DWL - has to give value to consum…\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m[P3] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-3.md › Examples in the Wild 3\u001b[0m  \u001b[2mcos 0.57 · bm25 6.8\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  When I joined Egon Zehnder (EZ), their services had a single price across all clients. We soon\r\n  realized that smaller companies, like startups, could not afford the full fee asked, while listed\r\n  clients were less sensitive to price changes. To address this, we introduced third-degree pricing\r\n  discrimination based on company size and segment. Startups and digital firms paid lower fees,\r\n  while large listed companies paid more. This allowed EZ to win projects with elastic clients,\r\n  while capturing additional revenue from less price-sensitive ones.\r\n\r\n\u001b[2msaved runs/search/20260922-184134-price-discrimination.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.044, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;35m━━━ 4. Ask-mode evals (standalone, cited) ━━━\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1.007, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki ask What are the eight behaviors of a great manager identified by Google's Project Oxygen?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[12.717, "o", "\u001b[1mAsk\u001b[0m · mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit · local\r\n\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  The eight behaviors of a great manager identified by Google's Project Oxygen are:\r\n  1. Good coach [S1].\r\n  2. Empowers (no micromanage) [S1].\r\n  3. Cares about people [S1].\r\n  4. Results-oriented [S1].\r\n  5. Good communicator [S1].\r\n  6. Supports careers [S1].\r\n  7. Clear vision/strategy [S1].\r\n  8. Technical expertise [S1].\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCitations\u001b[0m (answered_with_citations)\r\n  [S1] raw/leading-people-midterm-study-guide.md › Managing People\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mVerified quotes\u001b[0m (step 1, checked against the passages)\r\n  [S1] \"Google’s Project Oxygen (8 behaviors of a great manager): Good coach; empowers (no micromanage); cares about p…\"\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m8.25 s · 34.7 tok/s · MLX peak 5.87 GB · saved runs/ask/20260922-184148-what-are-the-eight-behaviors-of-a-great-manager-id.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.138, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki ask What cheaper service did my former executive search firm create so that startups could afford to work with us?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[13.257, "o", "\u001b[1mAsk\u001b[0m · mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit · local\r\n\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  My former executive search firm introduced a service that used \"third-degree pricing\r\n  discrimination based on company size and segment\" [S2]. This meant that \"Startups and digital\r\n  firms paid lower fees, while large listed companies paid more\" [S2]. This allowed \"low type”\r\n  clients, like startups, to afford the lower-priced version and start working with us [S1].\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCitations\u001b[0m (answered_with_citations)\r\n  [S1] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-4.md › Examples in the Wild 4\r\n  [S2] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-3.md › Examples in the Wild 3\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mVerified quotes\u001b[0m (step 1, checked against the passages)\r\n  [S2] \"To address this, we introduced third-degree pricing discrimination based on company size and segment.\"\r\n  [S2] \"Startups and digital firms paid lower fees, while large listed companies paid more.\"\r\n  [S1] \"while \"low type” clients, like startups, started to work with us as they could now afford the lower-priced ver…\"\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m8.23 s · 35.2 tok/s · MLX peak 5.78 GB · saved runs"]
[0.000, "o", "/ask/20260922-184201-what-cheaper-service-did-my-former-executive-searc.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.142, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki ask What is a sunk cost, and how did I fall into the sunk cost trap at a restaurant?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[14.936, "o", "\u001b[1mAsk\u001b[0m · mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit · local\r\n\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  A sunk cost is something that cannot be recovered and is irrelevant for future decisions [S3]. I\r\n  encountered this sunk cost trap at a restaurant when I forced myself to finish the pizza because I\r\n  had already paid for it [S4]. This situation is an example of sunk cost reasoning because the\r\n  money was already spent, and finishing the pizza did not change the fact that it was already gone,\r\n  even though it made me uncomfortable [S1].\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCitations\u001b[0m (answered_with_citations)\r\n  [S1] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-1.md › Examples in the Wild 1\r\n  [S3] raw/micro-final-cheat-sheet.md › Costs\r\n  [S4] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-1.md › Examples in the Wild 1\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mVerified quotes\u001b[0m (step 1, checked against the passages)\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  [S3] \"Sunk = cannot be recovered, irrelevant for decisions\"\r\n  [S1] \"This is an example of sunk cost reasoning because the money was already gone, no matter how much more I ate, a…\"\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  [S1] \"As we learned in class, sunk cost mistakes often involve taking future actions that rationalize past choices, …\"\r\n  [S4] \"I forced myself to finish the pizza just because I had already paid for it.\"\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m10.03 s · 35.1 tok/s · MLX peak 5.9 GB · saved runs/ask/20260922-184216-what-is-a-sunk-cost-and-how-did-i-fall-into-the-su.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
